{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>Torn I did not bring to you.</b> That is, it never happened among them that any animal was torn by an evil beast, in the manner of what is said (Genesis 37:33): ״חיה רעה אכלתהו טורף טורך יוסף״. And it is written (Nahum 2:13): ״אריה טורף בדי גורותיו״." "<b>I would bear its loss.</b> From the language of (Judges 20:16): ״אל השערה ולא יחטיא״ — that is, I was obligated to pay for it, and if it was lacking, it was lacking from my own. And so (1 Kings 1:21): ״והייתי אני ובני שלמה חטאים״." "<b>From my hand you would seek it.</b> The lost animal — for I am a paid guardian and I am liable for it, in the manner of what is written (Ezekiel 34:4): ״ואת הנדחת לא השיבותם ואת האובדת לא בקשתם״ — thus the prophet rebukes the shepherds of Israel for their flock and rebukes them for the things in which they are liable. And Jacob explains the great success he had with his flock, whether on account of his great vigilance or on account of the miracle, and so:" "<b>I was stolen from by day and I was stolen from by night.</b> I am liable for it, for a paid guardian is liable for theft and loss, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 22:11): ״ואם גנב יגנב מעמו ישלם לבעליו״. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: if theft, which is close to accident, is liable, then loss — all the more so. And Jacob explains here that he paid for everything from his own property — the loss and the theft, and even the torn animal, for which the guardian is exempt by Torah law, as it is said (Exodus ibid.): ״הטרפה לא ישלם״ — Jacob would pay."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "Printed citation reads נחום ב׳:י״ג; the Hebrew text as given cites this as Nahum 2:13 — retained as printed.", 1 "Printed citation reads מלכים א א׳:כ״א; normalized to 1 Kings 1:21."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:50.376038118Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}